  "question": {
    "text": "A cell of emf <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></math> and internal resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></math> supplies currents for the same time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></math> through external resistances&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math> separately. If the heat developed in both cases is the same, then the internal resistance of the cell is"
  },
